{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/1493171-have-we-allowed-ourselves-become-defined-by-labels","id":1493171,"title":"Have we allowed ourselves become defined by labels?","path":["Home","Onyx Path","Additional Discussion","Off-Topic"],"posts":[{"date":"2022-09-16T10:29:55-07:00","id":1493171,"title":"Have we allowed ourselves become defined by labels?","content":"In a post I commented that I was middle aged gen xer who might be waxing nostalgic about the past whom is getting more disillusioned about what is happening in the entertainment industry. It got me thinking that maybe that is the problem to begin with. That we define ourselves by our differences and not enough on what we have in common and how that could be harmful in the long run. I welcome any responses by the more articulate folks on the message boards.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>&quot;Power that needs to advertise itself is already losing&quot; Grand Admiral Thrawn <br /> <br /> &quot;Coronation Starscream? This is bad comedy!&quot; Galvatron","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27361-lysander","name":"Lysander","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar27361_11.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2022"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3523"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-09-16T12:42:37-07:00","id":1493178,"title":"","content":"I think your concern there is valid, but your approach needs to be reconsidered.<br /> <br /> On a fundamental level, humans operate by seeing the world through the lens of categories which with name with labels. Our whole conceptualization of the world is based on seeing patterns, sorting them into fuzzy categories, labeling those categories, and then interacting with how those categories interact with each other.<br /> <br /> This is unavoidable. The world is vastly too complex to interpret everything individually. Consider a flight of stairs. That is a category, with a label, that's important because we can tackle the process of walking up stairs much more easily that way. If you had to process each stair as its own unique object, and had to go through all of the mental steps of transitioning from one stair to another, a stairwell might as well be a wall instead of a useful way to walk from one level in a building to another.<br /> <br /> There is no functional way for typical human brains to ever overcome the way we're wired in this regard. We have to define ourselves by labels because that's the only way we understand what anything is. So the question cannot really be, &quot;have we allowed ourselves [to] become defined by labels,&quot; because to not be defined by labels would be to not be part of the human experience at all.<br /> <br /> As labels are necessary, the first step is accepting that into how we think about these sorts of topics. The way we construct labels is actually a very potent tool for good in humanity We don't have art without it. We wouldn't have the entertainment that sparked your comment in the other thread without it. We know our labels are shortcuts, and the fuzzy lines created by these broad concepts defining highly varied sets within them, is marvelous fodder for our creativity. Without these fuzzy borders between categories, paintings are just pigment on a canvas. Paintings evoke emotional responses in us because we can slot them into the blurred space between &quot;colorful canvas,&quot; and, &quot;face of a human.&quot; Poetry is rooted (calling it rooted itself is an example) in turns of phrase, idiom, and metaphor. Comedy and horror are both genres that are born from the expectations we construct about how the world works based on our labels, and then toying with those expectations.<br /> <br /> Of course, labels have downsides too. The indistinct edges of our labels make communication harder because they're ripe for misunderstandings as different people have different constructs in their mind about what does and doesn't fit into a label. We can take the edges of labels too far in either direction and be over expansive and include too much to retain a label's value, or overly prescriptive and end up with solid lines that harm our ability to see nuance. Labels can be used to harm especially as they allow us to be lazy and not think critically if we forget that labels are just mental shortcuts, not reality itself.<br /> <br /> Worse yet, as I lamented before, as we age are mental category-label system starts to calcify. Instead of regularly creating new labels and adapting old ones to fit new stimuli, we start to want everything to fit into the constructs we already have. We force things into labels that aren't good fits instead of making new labels for them, and that negatively colors our perceptions of these new things because we've naturally biased ourselves against them. New - especially difficult - concepts become offensive to our brains because it undermines the decades of work we've done to &quot;understand&quot; the world as being incomplete, and thus we are incomplete, which doesn't feel good. It makes us ever increasingly subject to manipulation by people telling us what we want to hear because it fits how our brains have learned to define the world. <br /> <br /> To get to a point in all this: instead of pondering if this process is harmful in the long run as if we could stop this process in some meaningful fashion in the first place, the important thing is to develop, encourage, and practice self-awareness. As we constantly make the world more complex, we're going to fall back on our innate biases to rely on categorization to handle it. The harmful path is just letting that happen. Push back against the fatalistic impulse that says that since we can't stop it, it's not worth it. Because we can't stop it, but we can guide it. And guiding it towards a good place is worth fighting for.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-09-17T00:17:08-07:00","id":1493205,"title":"","content":"Adding to what <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar617_2.jpeg');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"617\" data-vbnamecard=\"617\">Heavy Arms</a> said, labels can also be a powerful tool for mutual understanding instead of division. Actually, the argument of them as causes to the division is frequently used to further division, not understanding.<br /> <br /> Get your example. You define yourself as a Gen Xer. You <b>know</b> it is just a simplification, a useful tool instead of a hard and complete description of your reality. Yet, it is useful because it encapsulates a lot of stuff about you, your place in the world and in history, and that makes for an easier time understanding how those aspects of you come from context. So you're empowered to understand yourself more and to understand both those that are like you (Gen Xers) and those that are not (other generations).<br /> <br /> More importantly: while most people take their biases for granted and become more and more aggressive towards other generations, you actually <i>considered that you might be biased <b>because</b> of this label</i>, and this recognition made you more open to consider that these other media you don't like may still have more worth than you give them.<br /> <br /> The label didn't made you think differently from younger generations. You already did. It empowered you to understand why and decide what to do with it. If you saw everyone as just equal to you, then how would you understand, qualify and accept their differences?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-09-22T18:41:55-07:00","id":1493549,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Lysander</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/1493171-have-we-allowed-ourselves-become-defined-by-labels#post1493171\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">That we define ourselves by our differences and not enough on what we have in common and how that could be harmful in the long run.</div> </div> </div> </div>We can do both, and we should. A lot that is different between people is worth knowing, both on grounds of recognition of how to engage with people in terms of experiences that they have which we do not and just to generally immerse ourselves in the wonderful diversity of human life.<br /> <br /> I think if people ever really were distilled down to what they truly had in common with one another, the result would be rather generic. A lot of the texture lies in the differences.<br /> <br /> Although I'll be honest, I think without meaning to, a lot of the time when people say &quot;focus on what we have in common&quot;, what that ends up meaning is focusing on what can be adhered to within the standards applied by a society's most prominent group.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#00ffff\">I have approximate knowledge of many things.</span><br /> <a href=\"https://forum.rpg.net/index.php?threads/wip-xenoblade-chronicles-the-sword-can-see-the-future.835575/\" target=\"_blank\">Write up as I play Xenoblade Chronicles.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/158-isator-levi","name":"Isator Levi","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar158_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"17901"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-09-22T19:50:05-07:00","id":1493553,"title":"","content":"I assisted in giving a presentation introducing attendees to the Big Five Personality Traits. They are Openness, Conscientiousness, Extroversion, Agreeableness and Neuroticism (OCEAN). You can have a score between 0 and 100 in each. That multiplies out to a lot of score combinations that any individual can have. An important thing we emphasized was that two people with <i>exactly</i> the same trait scores can be completely different in the way the traits manifest in their lives. People really are complex and unique. A label is a conclusion. I go with my impression of people as I get to know them, improving the picture I have as I learn more.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Thank you for passing time with me in conversation. <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1480242-horizon-s-wild-and-crazy-homebrew-rules-and-metaplot-choices\" target=\"_blank\">My Hacks</a>.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26467-horizonparty2021","name":"HorizonParty2021","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar26467_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2020"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"610"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-09-23T08:36:42-07:00","id":1493577,"title":"","content":"I don't think the problem is being defined by differences, but rather being restrained by them. Recognizing difference between oneself and others is simply the beginning of individuality. Taking those differences as an automatic caste you may never step out of is just a problem.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/765-darksol-aeternium","name":"Darksol-aeternium","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar765_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"392"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-09-30T18:03:21-07:00","id":1494067,"title":"","content":"I'll admit, I feel a disparity between the title and the original post. From my vantage point, Generation X is not a heavily focused upon or consequential label; it has some significance for observing trends in affluence and the context of a person's formative years and how those things might influence current outlook and behaviour, but for the most part nobody really cares.<br /> <br /> Yet, if a person is to bring up a question of &quot;defining people too much by labels&quot;, I think there's a lot of background that would lead most people to look at that in terms of race, gender, sexuality. I think that's a distinction that a person should be aware of and handle responsibly.<br /> <br /> On those fronts, I think what I said before does have some relevance on an individual level, but I think of far more importance is what they mean on a systemic level. There are certain labels that matter because they allow people to recognise trends that disproportionately affect or outright target certain demographics, and provide the framework to organise in opposition to that. Like, if black people organise in protest of something like banking policies that deny them financial assistance, they're not necessarily looking to define themselves by the identity of being black, they're just using collective action against the fact that there are systems that will harm or deprive them either directly or as something emergent from history. Like, there are plenty of bankers who'll probably protest that they didn't deny that business loan specifically because the person was black, and maybe even mean it, but given the background it's not exactly better to deny the loan because the person lives in an area that is historically lacking in affluence if you look back on the historical origins of where that lack is.<br /> <br /> It can be a matter of a person saying that they don't want to be defined by the label, and that necessitates the person bringing up the ways that it is used against them so that those can be eliminated and the person can be that label on their own terms.<br /> <br /> Then there's the way that some people live in a complicated and atypical circumstance, and coming up with a word to articulate it is a helpful way of providing self-understanding.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#00ffff\">I have approximate knowledge of many things.</span><br /> <a href=\"https://forum.rpg.net/index.php?threads/wip-xenoblade-chronicles-the-sword-can-see-the-future.835575/\" target=\"_blank\">Write up as I play Xenoblade Chronicles.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/158-isator-levi","name":"Isator Levi","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar158_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"17901"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}